r1938519 | fkento | 2026-09-25 18:38:17 +0000 (Fri, 25 Sep 2026) | 46 
lines
 
 Bug 8429 - Text handlers see undecoded bytes even when normalize_charset 
is on
 
 The JavaScript, SVG and ICS handlers read their parts with decode(),
 which never charset-normalizes, so they saw the part's raw bytes: a
 UTF-16 .js reached 'script' rules NUL-interleaved, and a windows-1251
 .ics reached icstext rules untranscoded.  The HTML handler already used
 decode_and_normalize().
 
 Handlers
 - JavaScript, SVG and ICS now call decode_and_normalize() and encode
   the result back to UTF-8 bytes for rules, the URI detail list and
   child parts.  Binary handlers (PDF, Image, Archive) still call
   decode().  SVG, like Mail::SpamAssassin::HTML, sets HTML::Parser's
   utf8_mode when given bytes, so entities decode to UTF-8.
 - Mail::SpamAssassin::HTML passes <script> text and non-base64 data:
   URI content to child parts as UTF-8 bytes rather than Perl
   characters.
 
 Charset detection (Message::Node)
 - decode_and_normalize() passes an undeclared charset to _normalize()
   as undef instead of substituting us-ascii (the default is kept for
   normalize_charset 0).  Handler-produced parts, such as a file
   extracted from an archive, never carry a charset.
 - _normalize() accepts an undefined charset.  For undeclared or
   declared-UTF-16 data it calls detect_utf16() once; undeclared data
   that is not UTF-16 goes to the existing fallbacks.
 - detect_utf16() now decides both whether data is UTF-16 and its byte
   order, from the first 1024 bytes: a BOM, or NULs in at least a
   quarter of the code units at one byte position and few at the other.
   Otherwise it returns undef.  Previously it assumed its input was
   UTF-16 and, because of a misplaced *4, reported UTF-16BE even for
   plain ASCII.
 - A part declared UTF-16 with no BOM or NUL pattern is decoded using
   the declared byte order, or big-endian for plain UTF-16 (RFC 2781).
   Previously a declared UTF-16LE/BE was ignored.
 - UTF-16 identified by a BOM or the NUL pattern is decoded leniently,
   so a lone surrogate becomes U+FFFD instead of failing the whole part.
   JavaScript strings are UTF-16 code units, and obfuscated script can
   split a surrogate pair across two string literals.  A part declared
   UTF-16 with no other evidence is still decoded strictly.
 
 Undeclared UTF-16 with almost no ASCII and no BOM (e.g. CJK) is still
 not detected.  With normalize_charset 0, handlers see the part's
 original bytes as before.
 
 
